course: dictionary
lesson: 5940
title: Many meanings of mass
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the sentence tells you which one you need. Look at the words around it, which are its context. «A glacier is a large mass of ice that moves slowly downhill.» Here a mass is a big lump with no clear shape, not a science measurement.
explain: In science, mass is how much matter is in an object. «Everything that has mass pulls on everything else, and this pull is called gravity.» Mass is about the stuff an object is made of, so it is different from weight, which changes with gravity.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. If «a large lump of ice» still makes sense, you have it right. If you think «mass-produced» means a crowd of people, the sentence stops making sense, so look again.
fact: Mass is the amount of matter in an object.
fact: Mass is measured in grams and kilograms, using scales or a balance: 1 kilogram = 1000 grams.
fact: Mass stays the same everywhere, but weight changes with gravity: on the Moon you weigh less, but your mass is the same.
fact: «A mass» means a large amount or lump of something with no clear shape.
fact: «Masses of» means lots of something, as in «masses of snow» (informal). As an adjective, «mass» means involving very many people, as in «mass transport».
exercise: «A glacier is a large mass of ice that moves slowly downhill.» What does mass mean here? | *A large lump with no clear shape | A church service | A small, neat cube | A kind of gas
exercise: «Everything that has mass pulls on everything else, and this pull is called gravity.» What is mass in this sentence? | A colour that glows | *The amount of matter in an object | The main service in a church | A large crowd of people
exercise: «Igor Sikorsky built the first practical mass-produced helicopters.» What does mass-produced suggest? | Made from solid gold | *Made in very large numbers | Made once and never again | Made by one person in secret
test: Jayden says mass and weight are the same thing. Which is true? | They are always exactly the same | Weight stays the same on the Moon, but mass changes | *Mass stays the same everywhere, but weight changes with gravity | Mass is measured in metres
test: «Masses of snow fell overnight.» What does masses of mean? | Snow that is very cold | Snow that fell very slowly | Just one small flake | *Lots of snow
exam: «Jupiter grew cores about ten times the Earth's mass.» What is the best way to work out what mass means here? | Assume it means a big party | Guess without checking the sentence | *Read the clues in the sentence and check that your meaning fits | Ignore the clue about the Earth
